201
00:11:49,546 --> 00:11:55,128
[SPEAKER_02]: Peripherally, in regard to this... As we said, you can only see the nature of change once you stop interfering with the feeling.

202
00:11:55,148 --> 00:11:55,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

203
00:11:55,649 --> 00:11:56,649
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop trying to get rid of it.

204
00:11:56,689 --> 00:11:57,070
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right, yeah.

205
00:11:57,110 --> 00:11:57,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop indulging it.

206
00:11:57,750 --> 00:11:58,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

207
00:11:58,370 --> 00:11:59,771
[SPEAKER_02]: So, stop being unrestrained.

208
00:12:00,231 --> 00:12:03,673
[SPEAKER_02]: And stop trying to deny it through, like, overzealous restraint.

209
00:12:04,633 --> 00:12:05,654
[SPEAKER_02]: And hence, the middle way.

210
00:12:06,454 --> 00:12:17,219
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you recognize the nature of feeling is to endure, then you can recognize what that nature, or rather, you can then recognize what that endurance is also subjected to.

211
00:12:18,380 --> 00:12:23,042
[SPEAKER_02]: But in order to, as I said, in order to recognize this is subject to such and such,

212
00:12:24,323 --> 00:12:24,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Qualities.

213
00:12:25,204 --> 00:12:25,945
[SPEAKER_02]: Characteristics.

214
00:12:26,686 --> 00:12:28,228
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to allow it to be there first.

215
00:12:28,408 --> 00:12:28,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Enduring.

216
00:12:29,890 --> 00:12:34,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Not have a view that it's momentarily changing, arising, passing, arising, passing, arising, passing.

217
00:12:34,195 --> 00:12:35,436
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are completely separate things.

218
00:12:37,279 --> 00:12:37,399
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

219
00:12:37,519 --> 00:12:41,304
[SPEAKER_02]: It's there, remaining the same, while it is subjected to change.

220
00:12:42,615 --> 00:12:46,097
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you get to understand and feel the threat of the change.

221
00:12:46,638 --> 00:12:48,099
[SPEAKER_02]: Feel the dukkha of nature.

222
00:12:48,119 --> 00:12:49,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's... yeah, yeah, yeah.

223
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:50,400
[SPEAKER_01]: That's seeing the danger.

224
00:12:50,481 --> 00:12:50,961
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

225
00:12:50,981 --> 00:12:51,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, look, look.

226
00:12:51,681 --> 00:12:52,202
[SPEAKER_02]: It's changing.

227
00:12:52,262 --> 00:12:52,782
[SPEAKER_02]: It's changing.

228
00:12:53,943 --> 00:12:55,384
[SPEAKER_02]: That is dukkha, isn't it?

229
00:12:55,864 --> 00:12:56,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

230
00:12:56,265 --> 00:13:00,387
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why this has to be rooted in the danger right now of the feeling.

231
00:13:00,608 --> 00:13:01,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

232
00:13:01,368 --> 00:13:04,150
[SPEAKER_02]: Not after, not as a management, not as an explanation.

233
00:13:04,690 --> 00:13:07,913
[SPEAKER_02]: And as you said, even when people say, oh, this is impermanent.

234
00:13:08,553 --> 00:13:11,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Or the other one, the favorite, this too shall pass.

235
00:13:11,336 --> 00:13:11,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

236
00:13:11,956 --> 00:13:13,397
[SPEAKER_02]: That's another way of trying to get rid of it.

237
00:13:13,517 --> 00:13:13,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

238
00:13:14,038 --> 00:13:14,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

239
00:13:14,778 --> 00:13:18,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this... Why would you be telling... Why would you be telling to yourself that this too shall pass?

240
00:13:19,202 --> 00:13:23,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're basically trying to convince yourself that the current pain will go away, will change.

241
00:13:23,766 --> 00:13:25,247
[SPEAKER_02]: And why... Because you want it to change.

242
00:13:25,587 --> 00:13:26,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

243
00:13:26,328 --> 00:13:27,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you want current pain to go away?

244
00:13:28,089 --> 00:13:29,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Means you don't want it to be there.

245
00:13:29,390 --> 00:13:29,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

246
00:13:29,671 --> 00:13:30,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Means you're craving against it.

247
00:13:30,892 --> 00:13:31,052
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

248
00:13:32,207 --> 00:13:33,027
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

249
00:13:33,047 --> 00:13:38,930
[SPEAKER_02]: For many people, practicing the dhamma, then, becomes just another form of management, which is another form of denial.

250
00:13:39,010 --> 00:13:41,591
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not that... Or coping mechanism.

251
00:13:42,092 --> 00:13:42,932
[SPEAKER_02]: But in order to...

252
00:13:42,972 --> 00:13:47,354
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you're coping means you have to be affected first.

253
00:13:47,994 --> 00:13:48,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

254
00:13:48,234 --> 00:13:55,938
[SPEAKER_02]: If your practice is based on the level of coping, you cannot possibly uproot what's affecting you.

255
00:13:56,078 --> 00:13:59,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not so much that dukkha is anicca,

256
00:14:01,115 --> 00:14:04,978
[SPEAKER_01]: That my pain is impermanent and will go away, I hope, in the future.

257
00:14:05,018 --> 00:14:05,898
[SPEAKER_02]: It's anicca is dukkha.

258
00:14:06,058 --> 00:14:06,719
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the other way around.

259
00:14:06,739 --> 00:14:07,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's anicca is dukkha.

260
00:14:07,579 --> 00:14:10,381
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why we never mess around with the order of things.

261
00:14:11,081 --> 00:14:14,323
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always anicca, dukkha, the experience of anatta.

262
00:14:14,403 --> 00:14:16,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Is feeling permanent or is it impermanent?

263
00:14:16,644 --> 00:14:19,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Is what is impermanent pleasant or painful?

264
00:14:19,326 --> 00:14:22,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

265
00:14:23,088 --> 00:14:26,410
[SPEAKER_02]: And again, why are impermanent things unpleasant?

266
00:14:30,011 --> 00:14:36,354
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is that which is anicca, why is that which is subjected to change, why is it unpleasant?

267
00:14:37,534 --> 00:14:38,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Why can it be pleasant?

268
00:14:39,415 --> 00:14:44,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't want it to change like it is.

269
00:14:45,178 --> 00:14:46,218
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you might want to change.

270
00:14:46,238 --> 00:14:49,199
[SPEAKER_02]: As we said, when you suffer, you actually want the unpleasant feeling to change.

271
00:14:49,759 --> 00:14:51,280
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is change unpleasant?

272
00:14:52,901 --> 00:14:54,862
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter if it's changed for better or for worse.

273
00:14:55,322 --> 00:14:56,943
[SPEAKER_02]: Change in itself is dukkha.

274
00:14:57,343 --> 00:14:57,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

275
00:14:58,550 --> 00:14:59,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't have your say in it.

276
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:00,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

277
00:15:00,830 --> 00:15:03,351
[SPEAKER_02]: It's inconceivable.

278
00:15:03,391 --> 00:15:06,352
[SPEAKER_02]: You can tell yourself all you want, that you have a say, you're in control.

279
00:15:06,792 --> 00:15:13,573
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact is, you are subjected to change, because things change on their own, on their own accord.

280
00:15:13,593 --> 00:15:16,314
[SPEAKER_02]: It's completely inaccessible to you.

281
00:15:16,374 --> 00:15:20,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, you latch on to it, when it feels like it suits you, it suits your needs.

282
00:15:26,752 --> 00:15:35,976
[SPEAKER_02]: In a nutshell, the fundamental reason for any action, any kamma, good or bad, is latching on to change.

283
00:15:37,497 --> 00:15:44,040
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why, if you free yourself from that nature of being subjected to change, you free yourself from action.

284
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:48,862
[SPEAKER_02]: You free yourself from acting out of it, out of that ignorance, out of that contradiction.

285
00:15:49,482 --> 00:15:50,523
[SPEAKER_02]: You free yourself from kamma.

286
00:15:55,294 --> 00:16:05,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your actions... because any... yeah, your kind of intentions, let's say, are now no longer rooted in wanting change, wanting things to change.

287
00:16:05,319 --> 00:16:05,439
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

288
00:16:05,979 --> 00:16:09,381
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you say, oh, this too shall pass, what's going to happen then when it passes?

289
00:16:13,343 --> 00:16:18,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, there's this... whatever, I'm worried about impermanence, but it will pass.

290
00:16:18,906 --> 00:16:19,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

291
00:16:19,526 --> 00:16:21,507
[SPEAKER_02]: I tell to myself to help myself cope with it.

292
00:16:22,047 --> 00:16:23,048
[SPEAKER_01]: What's going to happen afterwards?

293
00:16:23,752 --> 00:16:25,633
[SPEAKER_01]: When this passes... Something else will.

294
00:16:25,833 --> 00:16:26,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

295
00:16:27,094 --> 00:16:28,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's going to happen then?

296
00:16:28,334 --> 00:16:29,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Something else or something else.

297
00:16:29,495 --> 00:16:29,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Something else.

298
00:16:29,995 --> 00:16:40,761
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you realize that, like, when people do use this coping mechanism, the management techniques, even on the pretext of dhamma practice, all you can affect is content of things that bother you.

299
00:16:41,301 --> 00:16:42,581
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this too shall pass.

300
00:16:42,862 --> 00:16:43,542
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that will pass.

301
00:16:43,562 --> 00:16:48,564
[SPEAKER_02]: Something else will take its place, while the nature of being subjected to change remains unchanged.

302
00:16:49,665 --> 00:16:51,246
[SPEAKER_02]: And you are fully under its thumb.

303
00:16:58,883 --> 00:17:06,366
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha talks a lot about turning away from libidity, turning away from feeling, I'd say.

304
00:17:06,946 --> 00:17:07,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Dispassion.

305
00:17:08,087 --> 00:17:08,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Dispassion.

306
00:17:08,987 --> 00:17:17,311
[SPEAKER_01]: So, turning away, fading away, becoming dispassionate, cessation, liberation.

307
00:17:18,852 --> 00:17:21,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, turning away from feeling.

308
00:17:24,534 --> 00:17:26,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you turn away from feeling,

309
00:17:27,047 --> 00:17:29,088
[SPEAKER_01]: By turning away from wanting change.

310
00:17:29,468 --> 00:17:31,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I was thinking.

311
00:17:31,350 --> 00:17:34,852
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not so much... it's not so much... You can't directly choose to turn away.

312
00:17:34,872 --> 00:17:38,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Turning away from feeling, because... yeah, well, because feeling's there.

313
00:17:39,795 --> 00:17:41,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you like it or not.

314
00:17:41,556 --> 00:17:45,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But... and turning away there, and that sounds like...

315
00:17:46,487 --> 00:17:49,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Pushing it away, you know, like, kind of not wanting it to be there.

316
00:17:49,388 --> 00:17:53,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, it can be taken... Like, same, when Buddha would say, oh, he doesn't tolerate.

317
00:17:53,469 --> 00:17:53,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

318
00:17:54,030 --> 00:17:57,071
[SPEAKER_02]: The, like, you know, the thought of, uh, lust or ill will.

319
00:17:57,651 --> 00:17:59,391
[SPEAKER_02]: People take that to mean, oh, you must deny it.

320
00:17:59,632 --> 00:18:00,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

321
00:18:00,352 --> 00:18:01,392
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you don't tolerate.

322
00:18:01,452 --> 00:18:04,433
[SPEAKER_02]: It means, I don't tolerate this, which is there, enduring in front of me.

323
00:18:04,453 --> 00:18:04,633
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

324
00:18:05,113 --> 00:18:09,515
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you try to deny it, as if, oh, this shouldn't be there, this shouldn't be there, you're overstepping.

325
00:18:09,615 --> 00:18:09,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

326
00:18:09,835 --> 00:18:10,075
[SPEAKER_02]: It means

327
00:18:17,101 --> 00:18:19,723
[SPEAKER_02]: Setting up in conditions that will result in turning you away.

328
00:18:20,144 --> 00:18:20,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

329
00:18:20,924 --> 00:18:22,466
[SPEAKER_02]: And those conditions are full understanding.

330
00:18:22,546 --> 00:18:23,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing this thing as danger.

331
00:18:23,566 --> 00:18:24,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Full understanding.

332
00:18:24,387 --> 00:18:24,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

333
00:18:24,807 --> 00:18:25,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing this thing as danger.

334
00:18:25,588 --> 00:18:26,829
[SPEAKER_02]: Full understanding of the nature of it.

335
00:18:26,889 --> 00:18:28,631
[SPEAKER_02]: And how did the Buddha describe full understanding?

336
00:18:29,091 --> 00:18:33,795
[SPEAKER_02]: By seeing the extent of gratification, by seeing the extent of danger, by seeing the extent of escape.

337
00:18:34,235 --> 00:18:34,996
[SPEAKER_02]: And by the way, he didn't

338
00:18:39,161 --> 00:18:40,422
[SPEAKER_02]: He used the same principle.

339
00:18:41,142 --> 00:18:46,125
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen... he talks about... he talks about seeing the gratification, danger and escape from the five aggregates.

340
00:18:46,325 --> 00:18:46,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

341
00:18:46,705 --> 00:18:47,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Six domains.

342
00:18:47,566 --> 00:18:48,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness.

343
00:18:48,866 --> 00:18:49,386
[SPEAKER_01]: The world.

344
00:18:49,827 --> 00:18:50,687
[SPEAKER_01]: The element of earth.

345
00:18:50,807 --> 00:18:51,568
[SPEAKER_01]: The gratification of the world.

346
00:18:51,588 --> 00:18:52,328
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

347
00:18:53,129 --> 00:19:01,133
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the extent of its gratification, as in the extent of its affirmation, either through acceptance or denial, you still affirm it.

348
00:19:01,193 --> 00:19:02,454
[SPEAKER_02]: That which you accept or deny.

349
00:19:03,834 --> 00:19:06,436
[SPEAKER_02]: And the extent of danger implicit in you doing that.

350
00:19:07,327 --> 00:19:16,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not so much turning away from feeling, as seeing that this is something I don't want.

351
00:19:16,729 --> 00:19:19,149
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it, yeah, not welcoming.

352
00:19:20,549 --> 00:19:28,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I don't want this, if I don't want to sort of step into this, and want to engage with it, and want to change it...

353
00:19:40,908 --> 00:19:44,409
[SPEAKER_02]: It's important to emphasize that it's not on the level of choice.

354
00:19:44,869 --> 00:19:46,629
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot be on the level of choice.

355
00:19:47,229 --> 00:19:51,310
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't have to choose to not want danger.

356
00:19:52,430 --> 00:19:55,451
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to start to learn how to see the danger.

357
00:19:56,111 --> 00:19:58,651
[SPEAKER_02]: Danger automatically means no passion.

358
00:19:59,291 --> 00:20:01,672
[SPEAKER_02]: No passion automatically means no desire.

359
00:20:03,512 --> 00:20:05,352
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no desire, there is no wanting.

360
00:20:05,852 --> 00:20:08,473
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no basis of wanting, there is no basis for craving.

361
00:20:09,117 --> 00:20:11,899
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no craving in regard to feeling, there is no ownership of feeling.

362
00:20:13,199 --> 00:20:17,942
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, that's complete dispassion, removal of any fire burning in regard to feeling.

363
00:20:18,382 --> 00:20:18,622
[SPEAKER_02]: Done.

364
00:20:20,483 --> 00:20:21,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding.

365
00:20:22,204 --> 00:20:25,486
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding the danger on the right level in regard to right things.

366
00:20:26,667 --> 00:20:31,730
[SPEAKER_02]: Not secondarily, not as a level of management, not as an afterthought, not as a linear explanation.

367
00:20:32,390 --> 00:20:35,272
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything fits beautifully, same as the modern science has proven.

368
00:20:35,901 --> 00:20:36,041
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

369
00:20:36,281 --> 00:20:41,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding why am I subjected to this danger, needs to be personal.

370
00:20:48,468 --> 00:21:05,197
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether it's the feeling you're contemplating, whether it's perception, whether it's the moods, whether it's sensual cravings, whether it's your will, for that matter, whether it's anything, understand the extent of gratification, the extent of danger will become apparent, the escape will be realized.

371
00:21:06,225 --> 00:21:08,506
[SPEAKER_02]: The escape means removal of passion and delight in it.

372
00:21:13,628 --> 00:21:31,994
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, not delighting in feeling, for example, is the same thing as not, like, wanting it to change.

373
00:21:32,715 --> 00:21:33,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know that whole

374
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:42,004
[SPEAKER_02]: It depends.

375
00:21:42,484 --> 00:21:51,009
[SPEAKER_02]: If you say, like, oh, you don't want it to change, that can also mean, like, you're interfering with it.

376
00:21:51,309 --> 00:21:53,010
[SPEAKER_02]: I want this to be more of what it is.

377
00:21:53,661 --> 00:21:56,083
[SPEAKER_02]: I want this to remain the same as it is.

378
00:21:56,183 --> 00:21:57,003
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, I want it to...

379
00:21:57,144 --> 00:22:00,246
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, you're implying another layer of that feeling.

380
00:22:00,946 --> 00:22:05,029
[SPEAKER_02]: So, although you think you're not wanting change, you're still acting out of change.

381
00:22:06,090 --> 00:22:07,211
[SPEAKER_02]: Change that you don't want.

382
00:22:07,371 --> 00:22:07,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

383
00:22:07,931 --> 00:22:12,154
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether you affirm, whether you deny change, you accept it.

384
00:22:12,234 --> 00:22:13,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It's revolving around it.

385
00:22:13,235 --> 00:22:13,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

386
00:22:13,475 --> 00:22:14,416
[SPEAKER_01]: You're revolving around it.

387
00:22:14,436 --> 00:22:14,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

388
00:22:16,007 --> 00:22:17,928
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to see the danger in the change.

389
00:22:18,048 --> 00:22:26,073
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter whether it changes, how quickly, when, for better or for worse, or changes just to remain the same a bit more.

390
00:22:26,194 --> 00:22:26,834
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

391
00:22:27,554 --> 00:22:30,556
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is seeing the danger in that.

392
00:22:30,896 --> 00:22:34,959
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in the content, not in the result, but in the nature of being subject to change.

393
00:22:35,259 --> 00:22:37,981
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like Sotāpanna's insight.

394
00:22:38,001 --> 00:22:40,702
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, the insight that qualifies you, that makes you Sotāpanna is,

395
00:22:41,867 --> 00:22:50,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever, whatever has a nature to manifest, as in to change on its own, to appear on its own, that's why it's impermanent.

396
00:22:50,209 --> 00:22:56,510
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that nature, not because of the content, because I analyzed its atomic structure and proven that it will disintegrate.

397
00:22:57,790 --> 00:23:01,051
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the experience of disintegration has manifested for you.

398
00:23:01,471 --> 00:23:04,052
[SPEAKER_02]: The experience of destruction has manifested for you.

399
00:23:04,072 --> 00:23:04,952
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not anicca.

400
00:23:05,712 --> 00:23:11,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the experience of destruction, I'm destroying these things, is of a nature to change.

401
00:23:14,014 --> 00:23:17,557
[SPEAKER_02]: The experience of construction is of a nature to change.

402
00:23:17,978 --> 00:23:21,120
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just basically seeing the pain of impermanence.

403
00:23:22,321 --> 00:23:22,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

404
00:23:22,922 --> 00:23:27,646
[SPEAKER_02]: If impermanence is seen correctly, it is felt unpleasantly.

405
00:23:27,926 --> 00:23:33,030
[SPEAKER_02]: If that which is seen correctly felt unpleasantly, that experience cannot be taken as mine.

406
00:23:33,050 --> 00:23:33,251
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

407
00:23:33,511 --> 00:23:34,952
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, there is no room for after.

408
00:23:36,613 --> 00:23:39,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It's funny, isn't it, how many people

409
00:23:40,710 --> 00:23:49,237
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I remember, you know, years and years ago, when I started meditating, and it was like, anicca was like, everything's changing, and it all just, like, feels really pleasant.

410
00:23:49,317 --> 00:23:50,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

411
00:23:50,698 --> 00:23:52,039
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a denial of reality, isn't it?

412
00:23:52,199 --> 00:23:52,439
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

413
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:54,621
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, now, there's no mother, there's no father.

414
00:23:54,901 --> 00:23:59,064
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I meditate to have this pleasant feeling of everything's just changing.

415
00:23:59,084 --> 00:24:01,066
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no pain, because everything is constantly changing.

416
00:24:01,246 --> 00:24:02,567
[SPEAKER_02]: It's exactly, it falls into those

417
00:24:03,187 --> 00:24:05,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Some have understood the wrong views that they would have described.

418
00:24:05,448 --> 00:24:06,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

419
00:24:06,328 --> 00:24:10,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody dies, nobody lives, everything is constantly changing, constant, constant state of flux.

420
00:24:10,229 --> 00:24:11,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing matters.

421
00:24:11,230 --> 00:24:12,470
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you even utter a sentence?

422
00:24:13,430 --> 00:24:13,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

423
00:24:13,610 --> 00:24:17,591
[SPEAKER_02]: If everything is constantly changing, your sentence will be meaningless by the time it reaches its end.

424
00:24:18,012 --> 00:24:18,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

425
00:24:18,332 --> 00:24:18,832
[SPEAKER_02]: But it isn't.

426
00:24:19,092 --> 00:24:21,553
[SPEAKER_02]: You get the meaning, which is clearly not changing for you.

427
00:24:21,753 --> 00:24:22,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

428
00:24:23,993 --> 00:24:25,754
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, you go around saying everything is changing.

429
00:24:26,643 --> 00:24:28,244
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the view that doesn't change for you.

430
00:24:28,364 --> 00:24:29,444
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's the view that endures.

431
00:24:29,864 --> 00:24:30,224
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

432
00:24:30,324 --> 00:24:32,305
[SPEAKER_02]: But you enable yourself to deny it.

433
00:24:32,345 --> 00:24:33,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything except for this view.

434
00:24:33,605 --> 00:24:33,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

435
00:24:33,885 --> 00:24:46,309
[SPEAKER_02]: You enable yourself to deny it by focusing on the content of things, making a meditation about the content, your mindfulness about staring at the content, so you don't have to look at nature and basically become aware of your responsibility for choosing to hold that view.

436
00:24:46,789 --> 00:24:47,269
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

437
00:24:47,329 --> 00:24:50,230
[SPEAKER_02]: Like that man who came to the Buddha and said... the Buddha asked him, what view do you have?

438
00:24:50,250 --> 00:24:52,171
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, well, my view is that nothing pleases me.

439
00:24:53,728 --> 00:24:57,453
[SPEAKER_02]: My view is that everything changes, constantly, every second of the time.

440
00:24:58,515 --> 00:25:01,319
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we can easily say, well, that really doesn't change for you.

441
00:25:01,660 --> 00:25:05,125
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, he said, oh, well, that view pleases you then, that nothing pleases me.

442
00:25:36,880 --> 00:25:44,267
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can even conceive anything then.

443
00:25:44,988 --> 00:25:48,631
[SPEAKER_02]: They will not be subjected to the danger of change.

444
00:25:50,512 --> 00:25:50,933
[SPEAKER_02]: They will not

445
00:25:51,568 --> 00:25:53,349
[SPEAKER_02]: I take in the nature of danger of change.

446
00:25:53,450 --> 00:26:00,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, because anything I conceive now is contained within this situation that we're seeing here.

447
00:26:00,215 --> 00:26:05,599
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, see, anything you conceive, say, anything, any thought that pops up in your head.

448
00:26:05,659 --> 00:26:05,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

449
00:26:06,320 --> 00:26:06,780
[SPEAKER_02]: There you go.

450
00:26:07,381 --> 00:26:07,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Popping up.

451
00:26:09,582 --> 00:26:10,403
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot create it.

452
00:26:10,743 --> 00:26:10,943
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

453
00:26:12,004 --> 00:26:12,845
[SPEAKER_02]: It appears for you.

454
00:26:12,965 --> 00:26:13,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

455
00:26:13,926 --> 00:26:16,948
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you conceive anything that it's of a nature to appear?

456
00:26:17,669 --> 00:26:17,829
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

457
00:26:18,387 --> 00:26:20,948
[SPEAKER_02]: To not shape the nature of abrupt change.

458
00:26:21,308 --> 00:26:23,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Because appearance is already a form of abrupt change.

459
00:26:23,309 --> 00:26:26,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, manifesting means... That's what it means.

460
00:26:26,230 --> 00:26:26,510
[SPEAKER_01]: ...change.

461
00:26:26,630 --> 00:26:26,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

462
00:26:29,151 --> 00:26:35,914
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize there's absolutely nothing conceivable that you can regard as not subject to change.

463
00:26:35,954 --> 00:26:41,156
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, you can't conceive anything as permanent, as the people would translate.

464
00:26:42,477 --> 00:26:43,337
[SPEAKER_02]: Or as not dangerous.

465
00:26:43,777 --> 00:26:44,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Or as not dangerous.

466
00:26:44,577 --> 00:26:45,338
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all implicit.

467
00:26:45,458 --> 00:26:45,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

468
00:26:48,160 --> 00:26:53,002
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say that the change you want, it's always like the forefront.

469
00:26:53,362 --> 00:26:54,763
[SPEAKER_00]: You want things in the forefront to change.

470
00:26:55,263 --> 00:26:58,584
[SPEAKER_00]: But change actually happens where you can't... Yeah.

471
00:26:58,664 --> 00:27:03,166
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean when I say you can only deal, aim at the content.

472
00:27:04,627 --> 00:27:05,227
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

473
00:27:05,327 --> 00:27:05,847
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the thing.

474
00:27:05,867 --> 00:27:11,590
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I want the change because you projected desire onto the content of what you want to change.

475
00:27:12,612 --> 00:27:17,553
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why sensuality kind of gets you locked on sense objects, and it's completely not in them.

476
00:27:18,313 --> 00:27:21,313
[SPEAKER_02]: It's in your dependence on that delight in change.

477
00:27:22,794 --> 00:27:27,334
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, you think it's in the things you can touch, see, taste, and so on.

478
00:27:28,775 --> 00:27:29,235
[SPEAKER_02]: But it isn't.

479
00:27:29,855 --> 00:27:31,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the slightest.

480
00:27:31,755 --> 00:27:34,116
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even a grain of it can spill into the material.

481
00:27:34,616 --> 00:27:35,416
[SPEAKER_02]: Inconceivable.

482
00:27:38,496 --> 00:27:42,057
[SPEAKER_00]: In any case, the change you're subject to, you try to conceive that.

483
00:27:44,425 --> 00:27:47,707
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you're not trying to conceive it.

484
00:27:47,807 --> 00:27:49,808
[SPEAKER_02]: You're conceiving it basically by overlooking it.

485
00:27:51,849 --> 00:27:54,350
[SPEAKER_02]: By putting it in the content, you have misconceived it.

486
00:27:54,850 --> 00:27:57,591
[SPEAKER_02]: Any form of misconceiving is conceiving is there.

487
00:27:58,872 --> 00:28:00,373
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you misconceive it to be there.

488
00:28:00,413 --> 00:28:04,414
[SPEAKER_02]: You misconceive pleasure to be contained in things, in response to this unpleasant desire.

489
00:28:04,474 --> 00:28:05,875
[SPEAKER_02]: You misconceive, misconceive.

490
00:28:06,195 --> 00:28:07,816
[SPEAKER_02]: Misconceiving means contradiction.

491
00:28:10,854 --> 00:28:12,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Any misconceiving means conceiving is there.

492
00:28:12,938 --> 00:28:16,164
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why... or you could say, conceiving means misconceiving.

493
00:28:17,126 --> 00:28:23,737
[SPEAKER_02]: And saṃsarāhānta is free from conceivings, free from contradictions, on that existential level.

494
00:28:31,075 --> 00:28:38,276
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, like, again, it's not like you have to figure out and sort of, you know, cast a net that's gonna scoop all the conceivings out.

495
00:28:38,517 --> 00:28:38,657
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

496
00:28:38,817 --> 00:28:43,918
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to stop entertaining passion through your ignorant acts, for which you're responsible.

497
00:28:44,138 --> 00:28:50,339
[SPEAKER_02]: There always... You can't do... You can't engage with lust, aversion, delusion, accidentally.

498
00:28:52,019 --> 00:28:54,060
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always on the basis of your choice.

499
00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:55,220
[SPEAKER_02]: Always.

500
00:28:56,357 --> 00:29:03,961
[SPEAKER_02]: You stop feeding those states of mind through your choice, and you will do so if you find a good reason to not do it.

501
00:29:05,342 --> 00:29:08,703
[SPEAKER_02]: You are then removing the basis for maintenance of those misconceptions.

502
00:29:13,746 --> 00:29:15,607
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why sīla has to come first.

503
00:29:16,027 --> 00:29:19,349
[SPEAKER_02]: Absolutely not negotiable in any shape or form.

504
00:29:24,973 --> 00:29:28,774
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why practice never leaves that container of sīla.

505
00:29:28,794 --> 00:29:31,395
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, oh, I've developed sīla, now I'll go and figure this out.

506
00:29:33,595 --> 00:29:37,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Develop sīla, understand it, that's it, that's what it means, figuring it out.

507
00:29:38,597 --> 00:29:44,198
[SPEAKER_02]: Once you understood the basis of non-lust, non-aversion, non-delusion, it's irreversible.

508
00:29:44,878 --> 00:29:47,739
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot engage with lust, aversion and delusion anymore.

509
00:29:49,260 --> 00:29:53,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Because to do that is always to basically want things to change.

510
00:29:54,163 --> 00:29:54,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

511
00:29:54,944 --> 00:29:56,585
[SPEAKER_02]: Always.

512
00:29:56,605 --> 00:30:10,293
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why both, like, uh, meritorious and demeritorious actions partake within kamma, partake within this principle of being subjected to change in ownership and you craving for it.

513
00:30:11,273 --> 00:30:14,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, there's a good action, there's a bad action.

514
00:30:15,840 --> 00:30:16,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, action is there.

515
00:30:17,621 --> 00:30:19,183
[SPEAKER_02]: Acting for change is there.

516
00:30:19,963 --> 00:30:22,665
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you act out of wanting this to change?

517
00:30:23,386 --> 00:30:27,769
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have no desire, no conceivable desire for this, I could change it.

518
00:30:28,680 --> 00:30:32,221
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it also ties in with the wrong view that you are the master controller.

519
00:30:32,741 --> 00:30:40,944
[SPEAKER_02]: See, you're subjected to change because of which you want change, because of which you act, and then change happens abruptly to you, but it's... you start regarding it because you acted.

520
00:30:41,104 --> 00:30:42,185
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it happens to you.

521
00:30:42,225 --> 00:30:44,005
[SPEAKER_02]: But why did you act in the first place?

522
00:30:44,025 --> 00:30:45,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you were subjected to things you couldn't prevent.

523
00:30:46,326 --> 00:30:48,287
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, your action is secondary.

524
00:30:48,687 --> 00:30:50,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Your ownership is secondary to that.

525
00:30:51,128 --> 00:30:57,370
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, basically, your ownership is fully within the container that you cannot conceive outside of, let alone own.

526
00:30:58,893 --> 00:30:59,539
[SPEAKER_02]: And control it.

527
00:31:00,573 --> 00:31:04,876
[SPEAKER_02]: But you forget that, and all you start seeing is me acting and things changing.

528
00:31:04,896 --> 00:31:05,817
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, I am here.

529
00:31:06,077 --> 00:31:06,638
[SPEAKER_02]: I am mine.

530
00:31:07,078 --> 00:31:08,739
[SPEAKER_02]: These things are... do belong to me.

531
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:09,880
[SPEAKER_02]: I am the master.

532
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:11,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, look, I can break this.

533
00:31:11,281 --> 00:31:12,082
[SPEAKER_02]: I can create this.

534
00:31:12,122 --> 00:31:15,164
[SPEAKER_02]: And I might not be able to change that or this, but I can change this.

535
00:31:15,545 --> 00:31:15,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

536
00:31:15,845 --> 00:31:16,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Some things I can change.

537
00:31:16,665 --> 00:31:17,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

538
00:31:17,686 --> 00:31:19,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, therefore, I'm still in control here.

539
00:31:19,408 --> 00:31:20,248
[SPEAKER_02]: That's more than enough.

540
00:31:20,408 --> 00:31:23,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, but if you were to ask yourself honestly, so which is more then?

541
00:31:23,411 --> 00:31:24,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Things you can't change or things you can change?

542
00:31:25,032 --> 00:31:26,213
[SPEAKER_01]: I was like, oh, crap.

543
00:31:26,633 --> 00:31:26,853
[SPEAKER_02]: Things I

544
00:31:30,746 --> 00:31:37,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Ability to change is fully enclosed within the vast sea of things that you cannot even conceive how to change.

545
00:31:37,212 --> 00:31:38,093
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's secondary.

546
00:31:38,333 --> 00:31:42,778
[SPEAKER_02]: And if ownership is... if mastery is secondary, it's not mastery, isn't it?

547
00:31:43,658 --> 00:31:47,442
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I'm enslaved by this, but I tell myself I'm a master of this.

548
00:31:47,502 --> 00:31:50,325
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm in this prison cell, but I'm free to walk up there.

549
00:31:50,365 --> 00:31:51,546
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

550
00:31:51,626 --> 00:31:53,468
[SPEAKER_01]: I can lay, I can stand, I can sit.

551
00:31:53,728 --> 00:31:54,549
[SPEAKER_02]: I am my own man.

552
00:31:54,569 --> 00:31:54,909
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm free.

553
00:31:58,556 --> 00:32:05,158
[SPEAKER_00]: In that door, like, you're only subject to that when you think you have a say in what you've been subjected to, isn't it?

554
00:32:05,718 --> 00:32:07,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that has to be implicit.

555
00:32:07,778 --> 00:32:08,618
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to be implicit.

556
00:32:08,639 --> 00:32:12,539
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you maintain these kind of implicit views through your careless action.

557
00:32:14,040 --> 00:32:22,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you have to stop maintaining, stop providing basis for those views through your, again, volitional choice of lust, aversion and distraction.

558
00:32:23,753 --> 00:32:28,154
[SPEAKER_02]: If you stop maintaining it, then, yeah, you won't be able to feed these implicit beliefs.

559
00:32:28,294 --> 00:32:38,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, yeah, if you are not already implying that you can master, that you can take possession of change, you wouldn't have any desire and lust towards wanting things to change.

560
00:32:38,596 --> 00:32:40,416
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be inconceivable.

561
00:32:40,436 --> 00:32:45,457
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why freedom from desire and lust is freedom from ownership, freedom from the sense of self.

562
00:32:47,697 --> 00:32:49,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are always trying to change something.

563
00:32:49,718 --> 00:32:50,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

564
00:32:50,718 --> 00:32:51,378
[SPEAKER_02]: Can't possibly.

565
00:32:51,738 --> 00:32:51,958
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

566
00:32:55,290 --> 00:33:00,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's actually... there's always the reason for the suffering, for the pain.

567
00:33:02,238 --> 00:33:02,599
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

568
00:33:02,639 --> 00:33:03,981
[SPEAKER_02]: There's absolutely nothing... nothing else.

569
00:33:05,282 --> 00:33:06,043
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the suffering.

570
00:33:06,083 --> 00:33:09,507
[SPEAKER_02]: Freedom from it is pretty much a universal goal.

571
00:33:10,248 --> 00:33:11,229
[SPEAKER_02]: Even for people who...

572
00:33:12,198 --> 00:33:17,723
[SPEAKER_02]: choose to go about it by proliferating their suffering, and lust, and aversion, and distraction, and chasing sensuality.

573
00:33:17,863 --> 00:33:20,386
[SPEAKER_02]: They still do it for the reason because they want to be free from the pain.

574
00:33:21,046 --> 00:33:22,668
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain of not having enough of what you want.

575
00:33:23,108 --> 00:33:25,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain of having more than enough of what you don't want.

576
00:33:26,832 --> 00:33:29,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Or as the Buddha said, pain of being...

577
00:33:30,686 --> 00:33:35,990
[SPEAKER_02]: Separated from the things that are desirable, likable, and pain of being united with things that are unpleasant.

578
00:33:37,251 --> 00:33:40,134
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything's perception, not just this thing.

579
00:33:40,174 --> 00:33:41,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

580
00:33:41,875 --> 00:33:43,397
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of the nature of things.

581
00:33:56,928 --> 00:33:59,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so we... the Buddha also talks about seeing the danger of the body.

582
00:34:02,480 --> 00:34:06,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a different way of talking about the same principle.

583
00:34:06,601 --> 00:34:06,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

584
00:34:13,804 --> 00:34:20,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratification, danger, escape from the eyes, sights, and everything dependent on that.

585
00:34:21,007 --> 00:34:21,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

586
00:34:21,607 --> 00:34:27,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Whichever aspect, particular aspect of it you might take, the nature is the same.

587
00:34:27,329 --> 00:34:28,669
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what you're supposed to discern.

588
00:34:31,908 --> 00:34:55,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Stay focusing on a particular aspect that will somehow reveal you the truth, magically.

589
00:34:55,485 --> 00:34:58,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the contemplation of death, quite, quite...

590
00:35:00,107 --> 00:35:02,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Straightforwardly undermines that, doesn't it?

591
00:35:02,148 --> 00:35:05,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, sharply.

592
00:35:06,069 --> 00:35:20,034
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it will be hard to... if you do the contemplation of that correctly, it will be very hard to then maintain all these misconceptions we're talking about.

593
00:35:22,435 --> 00:35:29,338
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, yeah, it might be a bit too much too soon for some people, if they haven't developed virtue beforehand.

594
00:35:35,507 --> 00:35:42,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't it this sort of... it's the same principle, really, talking about death is just that kind of abrupt change?

595
00:35:43,030 --> 00:35:46,691
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, just different way of arriving at the... at the discernment of the same principle.

596
00:35:47,471 --> 00:35:53,313
[SPEAKER_02]: Basically, death is that fundamental... doesn't matter how much I control, I can...

597
00:35:53,653 --> 00:35:55,934
[SPEAKER_02]: I can exercise, I can apply.

598
00:35:56,674 --> 00:36:02,476
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all within a very, very perilous space that is subjected to ultimate non-control.

599
00:36:04,017 --> 00:36:04,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Death.

600
00:36:05,089 --> 00:36:05,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Anicca.

601
00:36:08,572 --> 00:36:28,008
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if something is fundamentally, cannot escape, subject of abrupt change, whichever way, is that something you can regard as, oh, this is mine, dear to me, personal, intimate?

602
00:36:28,528 --> 00:36:29,409
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

603
00:36:30,130 --> 00:36:32,452
[SPEAKER_02]: All those attitudes require a degree of

604
00:36:33,686 --> 00:36:38,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, conceived permanence, conceived ownership, conceived control, conceived possession.

605
00:36:39,711 --> 00:36:44,535
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason why you can do that is because you're not seeing what it's all underlined by.

606
00:36:44,555 --> 00:36:44,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

607
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:53,603
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're not seeing what it's all underlined by because you're actively choosing to ignore it.

608
00:36:55,028 --> 00:36:55,808
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's avijjā.

609
00:36:56,388 --> 00:36:59,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Another metaphysical maya, veil of illusion.

610
00:36:59,789 --> 00:37:03,330
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a choice to distract yourself from the unpleasant truth.

611
00:37:03,350 --> 00:37:05,771
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly, because it's more pleasant to avoid that.

612
00:37:05,951 --> 00:37:06,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

613
00:37:07,251 --> 00:37:14,913
[SPEAKER_01]: And focus on the minor, you know, illusion of control, where I can get a bit of pleasure out of it.

614
00:37:16,373 --> 00:37:21,655
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the leper, the... Getting the satisfaction, yeah.

615
00:37:27,447 --> 00:37:32,409
[SPEAKER_02]: And investing his whole life in building elaborate fire pits where he can burn his skin and find enjoyment.

616
00:37:32,469 --> 00:37:33,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

617
00:37:33,029 --> 00:37:38,811
[SPEAKER_02]: It's usually, basically, building a home and life based on sensuality, which is pretty much everyone's life.

618
00:37:38,911 --> 00:37:39,171
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

619
00:37:40,652 --> 00:37:43,413
[SPEAKER_02]: While in reality, all of it is done because of pain and fear.

620
00:37:44,574 --> 00:37:46,554
[SPEAKER_02]: And in itself remains painful and fearful.

621
00:37:54,157 --> 00:37:56,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you choose to kind of... people choose to

622
00:37:56,660 --> 00:38:00,985
[SPEAKER_01]: To not look at that danger, that fear.

623
00:38:04,389 --> 00:38:08,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, actually, that is what they need to look at in order to free themselves from it.

624
00:38:09,015 --> 00:38:09,215
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

625
00:38:09,455 --> 00:38:15,642
[SPEAKER_02]: They choose to not look at it because that's the only way of freedom from it that they know.

626
00:38:15,762 --> 00:38:16,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

627
00:38:16,792 --> 00:38:17,952
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not freedom, isn't it?

628
00:38:17,972 --> 00:38:19,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's making... Yeah.

629
00:38:19,573 --> 00:38:20,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It's making it worse.

630
00:38:20,573 --> 00:38:20,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

631
00:38:20,833 --> 00:38:24,554
[SPEAKER_02]: It's making it... next time, making it even harder to look away from it.

632
00:38:24,574 --> 00:38:25,894
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, you have to double down.

633
00:38:26,014 --> 00:38:26,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

634
00:38:26,974 --> 00:38:27,495
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

635
00:38:28,015 --> 00:38:37,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether it's a bhutujana or an arahant, as the Buddha said in that sutta, Majjhima 13, I think, everyone wants to be free from affliction.

636
00:38:38,797 --> 00:38:39,417
[SPEAKER_01]: How does he say that?

637
00:38:39,437 --> 00:38:39,737
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

638
00:38:39,757 --> 00:38:40,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

639
00:38:40,798 --> 00:38:46,019
[SPEAKER_02]: But only when you reach, like, the level of samādhi and sensuality, developing the proper understanding of

640
00:38:47,538 --> 00:38:51,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you realize, ah, this is truly the beginning of freedom from affliction.

641
00:38:52,101 --> 00:38:54,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing less than this.

642
00:38:55,143 --> 00:38:57,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they realize, yeah, I always wanted to be free from affliction.

643
00:38:57,765 --> 00:38:59,546
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I was engaging with sensuality.

644
00:38:59,706 --> 00:39:00,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it was afflicting me.

645
00:39:00,927 --> 00:39:01,187
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

646
00:39:02,788 --> 00:39:03,949
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just that.

647
00:39:03,989 --> 00:39:07,612
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way I knew was to go down towards it.

648
00:39:27,600 --> 00:39:31,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like keep... keeping alive.

649
00:39:41,011 --> 00:39:45,915
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we don't want that.

650
00:39:45,935 --> 00:39:46,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Delete that bit.

651
00:39:46,476 --> 00:39:46,776
[SPEAKER_02]: Delete.

652
00:39:46,996 --> 00:39:47,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Backspace, backspace.

653
00:39:57,010 --> 00:40:07,078
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if somebody is overwhelmed, doesn't know how to practice, where to practice, what to do, it's just too much, all these aggregates, nāmarūpas and whatnot.

654
00:40:10,020 --> 00:40:12,822
[SPEAKER_02]: One thing you can know for sure is, if I

655
00:40:14,104 --> 00:40:30,181
[SPEAKER_02]: Willingly, mindfully, carefully, choose to not engage with things that are rooted in motivation based on greed, motivation based on aversion, motivation based on careless distraction.

656
00:40:30,962 --> 00:40:32,404
[SPEAKER_02]: I am actually practicing the dhamma.

657
00:40:37,206 --> 00:40:49,041
[SPEAKER_02]: But if I just go around mechanically and hoping for results to happen to me, and certainly ignoring my responsibility for my own choices, including choice of sīla, choice of practice, choice of restraint, well, then I'm not practicing the dhamma.

658
00:40:50,222 --> 00:40:52,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if I actually wear robes and do everything by the book.

659
00:40:54,047 --> 00:40:56,550
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not doing it in the right order, not doing it on the right level.

660
00:41:19,528 --> 00:41:20,609
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can spend the whole day.

661
00:41:21,650 --> 00:41:35,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, as a layperson, who doesn't necessarily has any, like, you know, more detailed understanding of the suttas and what to do, well, you can spend the whole day being mindful of motivation behind your actions.

662
00:41:37,383 --> 00:41:42,507
[SPEAKER_02]: And before you're about to do something, you, like, investigate, ask yourself, why are you doing?

663
00:41:42,688 --> 00:41:43,789
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the purpose of this?

664
00:41:44,369 --> 00:41:44,869
[SPEAKER_02]: Every little thing.

665
00:41:45,190 --> 00:41:46,131
[SPEAKER_02]: You wanna go out for a walk.

666
00:41:46,211 --> 00:41:46,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

667
00:41:48,297 --> 00:41:49,817
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter I shouldn't or I should.

668
00:41:50,418 --> 00:41:51,778
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is to know why.

669
00:41:53,639 --> 00:41:56,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, because, you know, the doctor told me I need to exercise.

670
00:41:57,280 --> 00:41:58,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that a valid enough reason?

671
00:41:59,720 --> 00:42:00,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe.

672
00:42:00,501 --> 00:42:01,341
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you determine it?

673
00:42:01,381 --> 00:42:04,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, is it rooted in wholesome or unwholesome?

674
00:42:04,842 --> 00:42:07,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it rooted in lust, aversion or distraction?

675
00:42:08,323 --> 00:42:10,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it rooted in necessity?

676
00:42:10,444 --> 00:42:10,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

677
00:42:10,684 --> 00:42:11,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you need to be alive?

678
00:42:13,107 --> 00:42:14,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you need to maintain your health?

679
00:42:14,768 --> 00:42:15,968
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that a goal in itself, maybe?

680
00:42:16,749 --> 00:42:22,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it because you have a greater purpose that health is required as a basis to perform that purpose?

681
00:42:24,053 --> 00:42:25,734
[SPEAKER_02]: To make you feel responsible for whatever you do.

682
00:42:26,554 --> 00:42:29,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you might choose not to go for a walk, or you might choose to go for a walk.

683
00:42:29,596 --> 00:42:33,197
[SPEAKER_02]: But either way, you won't be ignorant in regard to your choices.

684
00:42:33,217 --> 00:42:35,779
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't be ignorant in regard to your action by body, speech or mind.

685
00:42:36,499 --> 00:42:37,580
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's practice of dhamma.

686
00:42:38,660 --> 00:42:42,042
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether you know it or not, whether you call it like that or not, that is practice of dhamma.

687
00:42:44,939 --> 00:42:49,562
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing, like, whenever you have an answer, if you know that... Then you question it further.

688
00:42:49,782 --> 00:42:55,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the thing, like, if you... if you're going on explaining it further and further, you know you're already... That starts... Oh, hold on!

689
00:42:56,486 --> 00:42:59,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Why am I, like, so keen on explaining this?

690
00:43:00,328 --> 00:43:02,289
[SPEAKER_02]: Why am I... why was I triggered by this question?

691
00:43:03,069 --> 00:43:04,710
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I want to cover things up for me.

692
00:43:05,651 --> 00:43:07,392
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you covering up always?

693
00:43:07,432 --> 00:43:08,412
[SPEAKER_02]: It's your responsibility.

694
00:43:09,393 --> 00:43:09,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Always.

695
00:43:10,013 --> 00:43:11,434
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the responsibility is unpleasant.

696
00:43:14,186 --> 00:43:19,670
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why Buddha said ignorance will accumulate the burden, the burden of responsibility.

697
00:43:20,191 --> 00:43:20,731
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the weight.

698
00:43:21,512 --> 00:43:23,073
[SPEAKER_02]: The more you ignore it, the heavier it gets.

699
00:43:25,175 --> 00:43:27,957
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it seems sharper, more painful once you stop ignoring it.

700
00:43:28,417 --> 00:43:30,379
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, you start de-accumulating it.

701
00:43:30,999 --> 00:43:35,442
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you truly de-accumulate your karma, by accepting responsibility for it.

702
00:43:35,923 --> 00:43:42,308
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't de-accumulate your karma by, you know, making random offerings and cancelling the negative balance of your bank account.

703
00:43:43,468 --> 00:43:43,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

704
00:43:43,908 --> 00:43:49,272
[SPEAKER_02]: You're making good karma, but bad karma cannot be undone simply by performing good karma.

705
00:43:51,093 --> 00:44:00,299
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way to undo the bad karma is to fully accept responsibility for it and then free yourself through understanding of it, whereby it doesn't apply to you.

706
00:44:01,539 --> 00:44:04,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And there is no amount of action you can do to compensate for that.

707
00:44:05,202 --> 00:44:07,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Only freedom from action can free you from it.

708
00:44:08,967 --> 00:44:12,189
[SPEAKER_00]: A good kamma can be less pressing, but still pressing.

709
00:44:12,229 --> 00:44:13,249
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's a good kamma.

710
00:44:13,469 --> 00:44:14,270
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's still a kamma.

711
00:44:15,390 --> 00:44:16,271
[SPEAKER_02]: It's still a burden.

712
00:44:17,051 --> 00:44:18,011
[SPEAKER_02]: And it won't last forever.

713
00:44:18,672 --> 00:44:20,052
[SPEAKER_02]: The result of the good burden.

714
00:44:20,152 --> 00:44:20,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Pleasant burden.

715
00:44:20,793 --> 00:44:21,333
[SPEAKER_02]: Light burden.

716
00:44:21,893 --> 00:44:22,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Agreeable burden.

717
00:44:24,775 --> 00:44:29,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Ken, it's about seeing the danger in kamma.

718
00:44:30,378 --> 00:44:30,598
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

719
00:44:30,858 --> 00:44:32,899
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the danger in ignoring things.

720
00:44:34,357 --> 00:44:40,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the daydream turning away from clarity of your motivation, turning away from the clarity of things that are felt unpleasantly.

721
00:44:42,060 --> 00:44:43,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's why you would turn away from clarity.

722
00:44:44,702 --> 00:44:49,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because of some universe, like, mistake of the universe.

723
00:44:49,704 --> 00:44:49,884
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

724
00:44:50,044 --> 00:44:52,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's unpleasant to admit it first.

725
00:44:53,866 --> 00:44:54,286
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

726
00:44:58,247 --> 00:45:03,990
[SPEAKER_02]: And just because of that tiny little, deep attitude, whole mass of suffering,

727
00:45:04,692 --> 00:45:09,213
[SPEAKER_02]: For eons and eons and eons and eons to come for many people.

728
00:45:09,613 --> 00:45:10,314
[SPEAKER_02]: Just because of that.

729
00:45:12,534 --> 00:45:15,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's a bit complicated.

730
00:45:15,355 --> 00:45:22,677
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the avijjā, you know, the avijjā, the avijjā, is always in the present moment, literally.

731
00:45:23,597 --> 00:45:27,298
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not past avijjā, in the sense that you are not maintaining through the present avijjā.

732
00:45:27,698 --> 00:45:28,439
[SPEAKER_01]: No, yeah.

733
00:45:28,899 --> 00:45:32,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Present ignoring is where your responsibility is.

734
00:45:34,361 --> 00:45:35,402
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you can free yourself.

735
00:45:35,482 --> 00:45:40,386
[SPEAKER_02]: If your vijjā were truly past ignorings of things, that's it.

736
00:45:41,006 --> 00:45:42,247
[SPEAKER_02]: That's solidified in the past.

737
00:45:42,267 --> 00:45:43,308
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't uproot that.

738
00:45:43,649 --> 00:45:44,129
[SPEAKER_02]: But it isn't.

739
00:45:44,770 --> 00:45:51,795
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason that past ignorings still have the weight of ignorings is because you are now still partaking in the same nature of ignoring.

740
00:45:53,517 --> 00:45:55,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you're free... Yeah.

741
00:45:55,218 --> 00:45:56,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Same with sensuality, when...

742
00:45:57,000 --> 00:45:58,561
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know, maybe a different sutra.

743
00:45:58,641 --> 00:45:59,722
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it was the Leper Sutra.

744
00:46:06,696 --> 00:46:13,060
[SPEAKER_02]: And all the sensuality that he remembers from before that provided him great joy, was actually pain.

745
00:46:13,100 --> 00:46:15,642
[SPEAKER_02]: He sees the past sensuality as painful.

746
00:46:16,082 --> 00:46:18,303
[SPEAKER_02]: All the future sensuality that would have been, would have been painful.

747
00:46:18,363 --> 00:46:19,904
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot step outside of that domain.

748
00:46:20,044 --> 00:46:21,765
[SPEAKER_02]: It's fully enclosed within the painful domain.

749
00:46:22,886 --> 00:46:24,027
[SPEAKER_02]: And now that became apparent.

750
00:46:24,783 --> 00:46:28,386
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't mean like before I had the pure sensuality when I was engaging in it.

751
00:46:28,667 --> 00:46:28,847
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

752
00:46:28,867 --> 00:46:32,991
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, past is seen against the backdrop of the present understanding.

753
00:46:33,411 --> 00:46:36,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Future is seen against the backdrop of the present understanding.

754
00:46:36,274 --> 00:46:42,019
[SPEAKER_02]: Present is seen against the backdrop of the present understanding.

755
00:46:42,039 --> 00:46:42,460
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

